    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for any confusion. Let's address the technical aspects of your issue. A standard deviation (stddev) of 32.441 ms in your ping statistics is indeed quite high and suggests significant variability in the round-trip time of your packets. This could be caused by several factors, including congestion on the interface, a duplex mismatch, or even a faulty network device.\n\nCongestion on the interface could occur if the bandwidth is insufficient for the traffic load, leading to queuing delays and packet loss. This would manifest as high jitter and packet loss, as you've observed. To diagnose this, you could monitor the interface's utilization and compare it to its capacity. If the utilization is consistently high, you may need to increase the bandwidth or optimize the traffic to reduce congestion.\n\nA duplex mismatch occurs when one device is set to full-duplex and the other to half-duplex, or vice versa. This mismatch can cause collisions and retransmissions, leading to increased jitter and packet loss. To check for a duplex mismatch, you can use the command `show interface [interface_name]` on your router to verify the duplex settings on both ends of the connection.\n\nLastly, a faulty network device, such as a switch or router, could also cause these symptoms",
